> Retain the original cosine table and all its prefixes

The original nodes are0,.5,1,1.5,2,2.5,3 and the query is1.25. Every prefix of
length2 through7 is retained in its original order, with the native helper
computing cos at those nodes and the analytic cos(1.25) reference. Contrary to
the old explanation, the first two nodes0,.5 are not the nearest bracketing
pair: that first prefix extrapolates. Adding nodes need not monotonically
improve a polynomial approximation or its floating evaluation. The table reports
actual errors rather than asserting monotone convergence or a guaranteed number
of digits.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/neville_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto s = dm::algorithms::neville_cosine_study();
    std::print("| degree | actual prefix estimate | absolute reference error | adjacent estimate change " "|\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < s.degrees.size(); ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", s.degrees[i], s.estimates[i],
                   s.absolute_errors[i], s.successive_changes[i]);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.lines(s.degrees, s.estimates, "prefix estimate", {36, 105, 172}).points(
        s.degrees, s.estimates, "", {36, 105, 172}, 5).abline_h(s.truth,
                                                                {22, 163, 74}, 2).title("Original cosine prefixes: actual Neville estimates").x_label("prefix polynomial degree").y_label("value at x=1.25").save_svg("build/neville_original.svg");
    std::print("\nAnalytic cos(1.25)={}; first adjacent change is a placeholder with no predecessor.\n",
               s.truth);
}
```
