> Agreement is not an error certificate

Take f(x)=x(x−1)(x−2), sampled only at0,1,2. All three observed values are
exactly zero. Both the degree1 and degree2 prefix interpolants are therefore
identically zero, and their adjacent change is zero. Yet f(.5)=.375. The native
counterexample fixture evaluates those same prefix calls, so the difference of
two table estimates demonstrably does not bound the unknown-function error.

A classical interpolation remainder uses f^(n)(ξ)·Π_j(x−x_j)/n! under
appropriate differentiability and interval conditions; an actual bound requires
a justified derivative bound. Neither small table differences nor stable-looking
finite outputs supply that missing information. Neville and direct Lagrange
evaluate the same exact polynomial, but no universal floating-point stability
ranking is claimed. High degrees, clustered nodes and extrapolation can still be
ill-conditioned.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/neville_counterexample.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto s = dm::algorithms::neville_zero_sample_study();
    std::print("| degree | actual interpolant | actual adjacent change | true function value | actual " "absolute error |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < s.degrees.size(); ++i)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", s.degrees[i], s.estimates[i],
                   s.successive_changes[i], s.truth, s.absolute_errors[i]);
    auto p =
        dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot({s.successive_changes.back(),
                                      s.absolute_errors.back()},
                                     {"adjacent change", "actual error"},
                                     "computed value");
    p.title("Zero node samples: zero change, nonzero function error").x_label(
        "degree2 comparison at x=.5").y_label("absolute magnitude").save_svg(
        "build/neville_counterexample.svg");
}
```
