\alttext(These counters exclude validation/allocation, whose costs are included separately in the stated complexity.)

> References and conclusion

E. H. Neville (1934), “Iterative interpolation”, Journal of the Indian
Mathematical Society20,87–120, provides the historical method.
[The native direct Lagrange report|../../numerics/interpolation/lagrange_interpolation.mepml]
gives the equivalent interpolation polynomial through direct products. The
actual recurrence is useful without coefficients, while the cosine prefixes and
zero-sample example keep observed agreement separate from a certified
approximation error.

